## 1. Architectural Role
Deploys one Ocudu O-DU driving one radio unit, with an optional O1 NETCONF server for the NMS.
Install one release per DU; each attaches over F1 to the CU-CP of a [`firecell-cu`](../firecell-cu) release.

| `ru.split` / `ru.vendor` | Radio | DU | Fronthaul |
|-----------|-------|----|-----------|
| `"7.2"` / `benetel`, `comba`, `eridan` (default) | O-RAN 7.2x O-RU, optional M-plane (NETCONF call-home) | `odu_split_7_2` | `ofh.mode: dpdk`: SR-IOV VF on vfio-pci + hugepages |
| `"7.2"`, lab | Amarisoft UE simulator acting as the O-RU on the same node | `odu_split_7_2` | `ofh.mode: socket`: kernel raw socket on a host netdev (e.g. a veth) that Multus `host-device` moves into the pod |
| `"8"` / `aw2s` | AW2S Swallow RRH over CPRI, Swallow PCIe card | dynamic `odu` (split 8, `ru_sdr`) | `liboai_device.so` from the image, `libswallow.so` and `libratcm.so` from the host, and the host Radio Management Unit; the pod runs in the host network. The DU can write the Swallow file itself (`ru.sdr.aw2s.generate`) |
| `"8"` / `b210` | Ettus B210 over USB 3 through UHD | dynamic `odu` (split 8, `ru_sdr`) | `ru.sdr.b210.usb` mounts `/dev/bus/usb`; 20 and 40 MHz |
| `"8"` / `zmq` | no hardware | dynamic `odu` (split 8, `ru_sdr`) | ZMQ ports in `ru.sdr.zmq.args` |

**Timing and compression per O-RU type.** The DU holds the Open Fronthaul timing windows and IQ compression of benetel, comba, eridan, liteon, vvdn and mti (a generic set for any other type). With `ofh.vendorProfile: true` the chart names `ru.vendor` in the cell and stops rendering the generic set, so the DU applies the vendor's profile to every key not set in `ofh.ruOfh`; a key set there wins. The default (`false`) keeps the generic set every OFH DU got before, so upgrading the chart does not change a running DU. liteon, vvdn and mti have a timing profile only: no M-plane profile, so `mplane.enabled` must stay false for them.

Everything under `ru` and `txPower` is the **boot default**: a value the NMS sets over O1 (ru-type, radio-unit/sdr, tx-power) is persisted by the DU and overrides it at the next start. A radio the NMS selects that needs another attachment than the one this DU was installed with is refused and raised as the alarm `ru-attachment-mismatch`.

### 1.1 Components
| Component | Objects | Notes |
|-----------|---------|-------|
| DU | Deployment `<name>`, ConfigMaps `<name>-config` and `<name>-launcher`, Service `<name>` (O1 :830) | DPDK VF + hugepages, OFH netdev, or AW2S card; pinned CPUs |
| OFH netdev | NAD `<name>-ofh` (`host-device`) | `ofh.mode: socket` only |
| F1-U | NAD `<name>-f1u` (macvlan or bridge) | `f1u.network.enabled`; not with `aw2s` (use `f1u.bindAddress`) |
| M-plane | NAD `<name>-mplane`, Secret `<name>-mplane`, optional `mplane-dhcp` sidecar | dnsmasq leases the O-RU and sends option 43/129 (call-home) |
| Pre-flight | hook Job `<name>-preflight` on the DU node | checks VF on vfio-pci and hugepages, the OFH netdev, or the Swallow host libraries, Swallow XML and RMU socket; F1-U bridge, M-plane parent NIC |

`<name>` is `name`, or the release name when `name` is empty.

## 2. Install
```bash
# CU once, then one release per DU in the same namespace
helm install --repo https://accelleran.github.io/helm-charts ran-cu firecell-cu -n oran -f common.yaml -f cu.yaml
helm install --repo https://accelleran.github.io/helm-charts du     firecell-du -n oran -f common.yaml -f du.yaml
helm install --repo https://accelleran.github.io/helm-charts du-2   firecell-du -n oran -f common.yaml -f du-2.yaml
```

From a checkout of this repository, use the chart paths instead: `helm install du ./charts/firecell-du ...`.

`common.yaml` holds what the CU and every DU must agree on: `global.image` and `gnb.*`.
A DU's own file lists only what is specific to it: `duId`, `cell`, `cpus`, `ofh` (VF and MACs),
and optionally `mplane` and `o1`. The DU reaches the CU-CP at `cuCpAddress` (default
`cu-cp.<namespace>.svc.cluster.local`). Any Ocudu YAML key can be added through `extraConfig`.

Adding a DU to a running gNB is a new release; the CU and the other DUs are not touched.

### 2.1 Clash checks
Each DU Deployment records its gNB-DU ID, node, CPUs, VF and DHCP-served M-plane NIC in
`firecell.io/*` annotations. On install and upgrade the chart reads the other `firecell-du`
Deployments in the namespace and refuses a DU that reuses a gNB-DU ID, or that shares a VF,
a CPU or a DHCP-served M-plane NIC with another DU on the same node. `helm template` cannot
see the cluster and skips these checks.

"Same node" means both DUs pin `kubernetes.io/hostname` to the same value in their
`nodeSelector` (or `global.nodeSelector`). Any other selector can match several nodes, so
those DUs are not compared for VF, CPU or NIC clashes. A DPDK DU with pinned CPUs should be
pinned to a host anyway.

### 2.2 NMS Access
With `nms.rbac` (the default), each release grants ServiceAccount `nms` of the [`firecell-nms`](../firecell-nms) release in `nms.namespace` what the NMS needs for its DU:

- RoleBinding `<release>-nms` to the firecell-nms chart's ClusterRole `nms-ran-<nms namespace>`: reads of pods and their logs, ConfigMaps, events, and Deployments, ReplicaSets, StatefulSets and DaemonSets.
- Role and RoleBinding `<release>-nms-lifecycle`: get, patch and update on `deployments` and `deployments/scale` for Deployment `<name>` only, so the NMS can restart, stop and start this DU.

Every DU release adds its own pair, so the NMS can manage all the DUs in the namespace. The NMS and the DUs can be installed in either order: the bindings grant nothing until the NMS's ServiceAccount and ClusterRole exist.

Every install and upgrade also writes ConfigMap `<release>-release-info` (label `firecell.io/release-info: "true"`) in the release namespace, for the NMS support bundles and drift checks, since the NMS reads no Helm release Secret. `release.json` holds the release, namespace, chart (`<name>-<version>`), appVersion, revision, render time (`renderedAt`, UTC), a SHA-256 digest of the values (`valuesDigest`) and the image of every container the chart renders (`images`, keyed `<workload>/<container>`, taken from the rendered pods themselves). `history.json` keeps up to 20 earlier `release.json` records, newest first, read back from the live ConfigMap with `lookup`; it starts empty after `helm template` or a client-side dry run. Neither holds values content or Secret data in clear; but `valuesDigest` is an unsalted SHA-256 over all values, so with a chart-made O1 NETCONF password it also covers that password (prefer `existingSecret`). `helm rollback` reapplies the stored manifest of the target revision, so the record and its history roll back with it.

### 2.3 O1 NETCONF Login
With `o1.enabled`, the DU takes its NETCONF login from a Secret mounted read-only at `/etc/ocudu/o1-netconf` (an RC3 or later image; an older image ignores it):

| Setup | Values | Login |
|-------|--------|-------|
| None (default) | nothing | Lab login `netconf`/`netconf` in group `operator`, with a start-up WARNING |
| One Secret per RAN namespace (recommended) | `o1.netconf.existingSecret: <secret>` | User and password from the Secret (keys `username`, `password`); the lab login is removed |
| Chart-made Secret | `o1.netconf.password` and/or `o1.netconf.authorizedKeys`, optional `o1.netconf.username` | As above, from Secret `<name>-o1-netconf` |

- **Image version.** Only an RC3 or later image reads the Secret and these settings. An RC2 or older image ignores all `o1.netconf` values: whatever Secret is mounted, it keeps the default login `netconf`/`netconf` with access control (NACM) off, so that login can change everything, users included, and it listens on every address, Multus interfaces included. The chart refuses any of these values (a Secret, `group`, `nacm`, `listenAddress`, `keepRsaHostKey`, `requireSecret`) when the element's image tag is one known to ignore them (`0.1.0`, `0.2.0`, with or without `v`); it does not judge other tags (`pr-*`, `edge`, digests). Rolling the image back to RC2 reopens that login. During an image rollback to RC2, set `o1.enabled: false`; switching O1 off is the only safe option. RC2 ignores the Secret, the access rules and the listen address: it opens `netconf`/`netconf` with access control off on every address, the Multus interfaces included, where a NetworkPolicy does not apply (on an AW2S DU nothing but the node firewall does).
- **Chart rollback** (`helm rollback` to the released chart 0.2.1, with the RC3 image kept): the Secret mount, `O1_REQUIRE_SECRET`, the listen address and `keepRsaHostKey` disappear.
  - An element that used a Secret keeps NETCONF off through its mark, if it still finds its node folder. A DU whose node folder is lost has no mark and opens `netconf`/`netconf`.
  - An element that ran with `keepRsaHostKey` switches to its ECDSA key (or makes one) and retires the RSA file: the host-key change today's NMS refuses until its pods restart.
  - NETCONF listens on every address again. Chart 0.2.1 cannot set `O1_REQUIRE_SECRET=false`, so going back to the lab login needs a hand edit. Prefer switching O1 off (`o1.enabled: false`) for the time of the chart rollback.
- **Chart-made password.** Prefer `existingSecret` (one Secret per RAN namespace, made outside the chart). A password in `o1.netconf.password` is also kept in the Helm release record and in the values files used, so it ends up wherever those go. The pod annotation that rolls the pod carries an unsalted SHA-256 of it, which anyone who can read pods can brute-force offline, and so does `valuesDigest` in ConfigMap `<release>-release-info` (an unsalted SHA-256 over all values, the password included): if you use it, use a long random value.
- The Secret is mounted with `optional: true`: a deleted Secret does not stop the pod, only NETCONF. The chart sets `O1_REQUIRE_SECRET=true` whenever it mounts a Secret, so a lost Secret keeps NETCONF off instead of bringing the lab login back.
- The element reads the Secret at start only. A chart-made Secret rolls the pod when its values change; after changing an `existingSecret`, restart the pod in a maintenance window.
- An element that has used a Secret keeps NETCONF off when the Secret is dropped from the values. That mark lives in the element's node folder only: a lost folder, or a pod moved to another node, loses it. To drop a Secret, always set `o1.netconf.requireSecret: false` and remove the Secret values.
- The NMS needs the same user and password (firecell-nms `backend.extraEnv`); restart its backend pods after a change.
- `o1.netconf.group` (`O1_NETCONF_GROUP`), `o1.netconf.nacm` (`O1_NACM_ENABLED`) and `o1.netconf.keepRsaHostKey` (`O1_HOSTKEY_KEEP_RSA`: keep the shared RSA image host key instead of a per-element ECDSA key, for a site that cannot restart or re-pin its NMS yet; it works before or after the first RC3 start, and on a new element it writes the shared image key to the node folder) are passed only when set. The lab login's group (`O1_LAB_LOGIN_GROUP`, default `operator`) is an image setting the chart does not expose.
- NETCONF listens on the pod IP (`NETCONF_LISTEN_ADDRESS`), not on every address, so it is not reachable on the Multus interfaces, which a NetworkPolicy does not filter. The NMS reaches it through the Service as before. `o1.netconf.listenAddress` overrides it (`0.0.0.0`: all addresses, the image default). `kubectl port-forward` to port 830 no longer works, since it connects to the pod's localhost. With `ru.vendor: aw2s` the DU is in the host network, so its pod IP is the node's address; limit port 830 to the NMS with the node firewall.

## 3. Node prerequisites
- `ofh.mode: dpdk`: VFs created and bound to `vfio-pci` before kubelet starts; one VF per DU (`ofh.vfPciAddr`).
- `ofh.mode: dpdk`: 2 MiB hugepages, at least `hugepages2Mi` per DU on the node (`vm.nr_hugepages`); restart kubelet after changing them.
- `ofh.mode: socket`: the netdev `ofh.interface` exists on the node before the DU starts (MTU and offloads set on the host), and Multus has the `host-device` plugin.
- `ru.vendor: aw2s`: Swallow PCIe card with its kernel module, and `libswallow.so` and `libratcm.so` in `ru.sdr.aw2s.hostLibDir` (the device library `liboai_device.so` is taken from the image first), the Swallow XML in `ru.sdr.aw2s.configDir` (writable with `generate`), and the host Radio Management Unit running (its socket is in `/tmp`). The DU uses the host network, so only one AW2S DU per node can keep the default O2 (9090) and O1 (830) ports.
- Isolated CPUs for `cpus.timing`, `cpus.txrx` (OFH) or `cpus.ru` (split 8), and `cpus.pool`.
- `f1u.network.cniType: bridge`: the Linux bridge `f1u.network.master` exists on the node.
- Multus with the macvlan and static IPAM plugins when `mplane.enabled`.
- One O-RU per M-plane parent NIC when `mplane.dhcp.enabled` (one DHCP server per segment).
- An image that ships `/usr/local/bin/entrypoint-o1.sh` when `o1.enabled`.

